  "user_handle": "anchabadze",
  "body": "## Description:\nThe protocol will be deployed on Optimism (an L2 network), but the `ChainlinkOracle` implementation does not include a check for the L2 sequencer's uptime status. This is a critical omission because L2 sequencers can experience downtime, during which Chainlink price feeds on L2 cannot be updated with fresh prices from the mainnet. The vulnerability exists in the `_getQuote()` function in `ChainlinkOracle.sol (EulerChainlinkOracle)`:\n```solidity\nfunction _getQuote(uint256 inAmount, address _base, address _quote) internal view override returns (uint256) {\n    bool inverse = ScaleUtils.getDirectionOrRevert(_base, base, _quote, quote);\n\n    (, int256 answer,, uint256 updatedAt,) = AggregatorV3Interface(feed).latestRoundData();\n    if (answer <= 0) revert Errors.PriceOracle_InvalidAnswer();\n    uint256 staleness = block.timestamp - updatedAt;\n    if (staleness > maxStaleness) revert Errors.PriceOracle_TooStale(staleness, maxStaleness);\n\n    uint256 price = uint256(answer);\n    return ScaleUtils.calcOutAmount(inAmount, price, scale, inverse);\n}\n```\nWhile the code checks for price staleness via `maxStaleness`, it does not verify whether the L2 sequencer was down.\nAccording to Chainlink's official documentation, protocols deployed on L2 networks should implement sequencer uptime checks to prevent exploitation during and immediately after sequencer downtime.\nhttps://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/l2-sequencer-feeds\n\n## Impact:\nAfter sequencer restart, prices may pass staleness checks but still be outdated\nAttackers can interact with the protocol using prices that don't reflect true market values\nThe grace period (determined by `maxStaleness`) provides a predictable exploitation window\n\n## Recommended Mitigation:\nAdd a check if the L2 Sequencer is down",
  "proof_of_concept": "Add this in `C4PoC.t.sol` and run `forge test --mt test_L2SequencerDowntimeVulnerability -vvv`\n```solidity\nfunction test_L2SequencerDowntimeVulnerability() public {\n        // Setup initial oracle price: ETH = $2000\n        uint256 initialPrice = 2000e8; // $2000 with 8 decimals\n        chainlinkAggregator.setRoundData(\n            1, // roundId\n            int256(initialPrice),\n            block.timestamp,\n            block.timestamp,\n            1 // answeredInRound\n        );\n\n        // Verify oracle returns initial price\n        uint256 price1 = chainlinkOracle.getQuote(1e18, _mockBaseAsset, _mockQuoteAsset);\n        console.log(\"\\n=== INITIAL STATE ===\");\n        console.log(\"Initial price from oracle:\", price1);\n\n        // CRITICAL: Simulate sequencer downtime\n        // Move time forward by 2 hours (sequencer is down)\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 2 hours);\n\n        // During downtime, real market price drops to $1500\n        // But Chainlink oracle on L2 cannot update because sequencer is down\n        // When sequencer comes back, first update happens\n        uint256 newPrice = 1500e8; // $1500 - real market price after crash\n\n        // Set new price with timestamp that's within maxStaleness (1 hour)\n        // This simulates first price update after sequencer restart\n        chainlinkAggregator.setRoundData(\n            2,\n            int256(newPrice),\n            block.timestamp - 30 minutes, // Within maxStaleness (1 hour)\n            block.timestamp - 30 minutes,\n            2\n        );\n\n        // VULNERABILITY: Oracle accepts this price without checking if sequencer was down\n        // The protocol has NO way to know that:\n        // 1. Sequencer was down for 2 hours\n        // 2. This price is the first update after sequencer restart\n        // 3. Price may have changed significantly during downtime\n        uint256 price2 = chainlinkOracle.getQuote(1e18, _mockBaseAsset, _mockQuoteAsset);\n\n        console.log(\"\\n=== VULNERABILITY DEMONSTRATED ===\");\n        console.log(\"Price after sequencer restart:\", price2);\n        console.log(\"Price accepted without sequencer uptime check!\");\n        console.log(\"Sequencer was down for: 2 hours\");\n        console.log(\"Price staleness: 30 minutes (within maxStaleness)\");\n\n        // Verify that oracle accepted the price\n        assertGt(price2, 0, \"Oracle returns price without sequencer check\");\n        assertEq(price2, 1500e18, \"Oracle returns updated price\");\n    }\n```",
